<Guide slug="remove-password-from-pdf-statement">
  <p>Banks, payroll providers and tax offices often email statements as password-protected PDFs. That protects the file on its way to you. Once it is safely on your own computer, the password mostly gets in the way: you type it every time you open the file, search cannot look inside it, and you cannot merge a year of statements into one document.</p>
  <p>If the document is yours and you know the password, saving an unprotected copy is straightforward. All three methods below keep the file on your device — worth insisting on, given what a bank statement contains.</p>

  <h2>Method 1: in your browser, with ShyPDF</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/unlock-pdf/">Unlock PDF</a> and choose the file.</li>
    <li>Type the password, and confirm that the document is yours to unlock.</li>
    <li>Click “Unlock PDF”. A copy that opens without a password is saved to your downloads folder.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The file and the password are processed in your browser by qpdf, an open-source PDF library, and neither is uploaded. The copy is otherwise identical to the original: text stays selectable, and nothing is re-compressed.</p>

  <h2>Method 2: print to PDF</h2>
  <p>Open the PDF in Chrome or Edge, enter the password, then choose <i>Print</i> and set the destination to <i>Save as PDF</i>. The saved copy has no password. This needs no tools at all, but it only works if the document permits printing, and the result is a re-generated file: bookmarks and links are usually lost, and the file size can change.</p>

  <h2>Method 3: Preview on a Mac</h2>
  <p>Open the file in Preview and enter the password. Choose <i>File → Export…</i>, click <i>Permissions…</i> (or <i>Show Details</i>, depending on your macOS version) and make sure no password is required to open the document, then save under a new name.</p>

  <h2>What none of these will do</h2>
  <p>All three methods require the password. Neither ShyPDF nor your browser can open a PDF whose password you do not know, and we do not offer password cracking. If you have lost the password to a statement, ask the sender: banks usually document the scheme they use (often a combination of your date of birth, postcode or customer number) or can issue the document again.</p>

  <h2>Storing the unlocked copies safely</h2>
  <p>Removing the password removes a layer of protection, so put something in its place:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep unlocked statements on a device with full-disk encryption turned on (FileVault on a Mac, BitLocker or Device Encryption on Windows). Phones are encrypted by default when they have a passcode.</li>
    <li>If you need to send one to an accountant or a landlord, <a href="/protect-pdf/">add your own password</a> — one you choose, shared through a different channel than the file.</li>
    <li>Sending only part of a statement? <a href="/split-pdf/">Extract the pages</a> that are needed rather than sharing the whole thing.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
